<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════
     § 05 · THE REFRAME
══════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="section">
  <h2 class="cs-h2-wide">Everything is a discovery.</h2>
  <p class="body-text">The old product treated Ideas, Trends, and Customer segments as separate destinations. They're all expressions of the same underlying PM job — making sense of evidence to decide what to build. Discovery 2.0 unifies them under two modes of entry.</p>

  <div class="reframe-split">
    <div class="rf-before">
      <span class="rf-tag">Before</span>
      <div class="rf-title">Three spaces, no connection</div>
      <div class="rf-item">Ideas backlog — a flat list with no discovery path</div>
      <div class="rf-item">Trends — isolated view, no link to action</div>
      <div class="rf-item">Customer 360 — same evidence, different packaging</div>
      <div class="rf-item">Discovery — most powerful, barely accessed</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rf-after">
      <span class="rf-tag">After — Discovery 2.0</span>
      <div class="rf-title">Two mindsets, one flow</div>
      <div class="rf-item"><strong>"I want to explore"</strong> — broad or no query; biggest gaps surfaced by evidence and ARR</div>
      <div class="rf-item"><strong>"I know what I'm looking for"</strong> — targeted query; ranked themes with full evidence trail</div>
      <div class="rf-item">Every theme is actionable — create, enrich, or track without leaving the flow</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

